One dusk, a child named Lira asked the tree for a story to soften a stubborn hunger in the village. The tree rustled its leaves and told of a time before tides, when rivers braided with the breath of stars. In that time, a traveler carried a flute carved from a storm-swept branch. When the traveler played, storms gathered, rains fell in generous cascades, and seeds learned to drink the rain with their roots, becoming trees that could hear the sky.

But the traveler vanished into a thunderhead, leaving only the flute’s echo. The tree warned that every song leaves a mark, and every mark seeks a listener. Lira understood: to bring rain back, one must listen to the whispers of the well, and answer with a song that remembers where it began.

